          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now I have excellent precision on all three axes. Let me compile all the measurements using the **0.5 threshold** (half-intensity boundary) as the cavity wall:\n\n**Summary of measurements through the cavity center (x=29, y=30, z=35):**\n\n| Axis | Scan line | Entry boundary (0.5) | Exit boundary (0.5) | Midpoint | Half-width (radius) |\n|------|-----------|----------------------|---------------------|----------|---------------------|\n| X | y=30, z=35 | x\u224823.5 | x\u224834.5 | **29.0** | **5.5** |\n| Y | x=29, z=35 | y\u224824.5 | y\u224835.5 | **30.0** | **5.5** |\n| Z | x=29, y=30 | z\u224829.5 | z\u224840.5 | **35.0** | **5.5** |\n\nAll three axes give **identical radius = 5.5 voxels** and a clean center at **(29, 30, 35)**. The cavity is a perfect sphere.\n\n---\n\n## Results\n\n### (a) Cavity Center (voxel space)\n**(x, y, z) = (29, 30, 35)**\n\nThis was determined by finding where the Density field drops from 1 \u2192 0 along all three principal axes through the volume. The 0.5 boundary transitions are symmetric around this point on every axis.\n\n### (b) Cavity Radius\n**\u2248 5.5 voxels**\n\nMeasured as the half-width of the zero-density region along each axis (X, Y, and Z all independently yield the same value of 5.5), confirming the cavity is a perfect sphere with radius **5.5 voxels**.\n\n**Cross-check:** The outer solid sphere also has radius \u2248 21 voxels (centered at \u2248(32, 32, 32), edges at ~11 and ~53 per axis), so the cavity is clearly an internal feature offset from the outer sphere's center, confirming it is a genuinely *hidden* internal cavity."
